The attached image displays flow cytometry histograms illustrating the expression of costimulatory molecules CD40, CD80, and CD86 on bone marrow-derived dendritic cells (BMDCs). The gray dotted lines represent BMDCs treated with medium only (control), while the black solid lines represent BMDCs incubated with Nano-11. The significant rightward shift of the black curves, particularly for CD80 and CD86, demonstrates that Nano-11 induces a marked increase in the expression of these costimulatory molecules, indicating dendritic cell activation. This activation is a key mechanism by which Nano-11 enhances the immune response, as discussed in the PDF.

The demonstrated ability of Nano-11 to potently activate dendritic cells, as evidenced by the upregulation of costimulatory molecules CD40, CD80, and CD86, significantly contributes to its application in vaccine development. This mechanism is crucial for an effective adjuvant, as activated dendritic cells are essential for initiating robust adaptive immune responses by presenting antigens and providing critical signals to T cells. Thus, Nano-11 holds promise for enhancing the immunogenicity of subunit vaccines, leading to more potent and long-lasting protective immunity.